<id/17/1768/#concept> a <http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E55_Type>, skos:Concept ;
    rdfs:label "Bark"@en ;
    skos:broadMatch <customs-1866/forest_product/#concept> ;
    skos:broader <id/grp/grp_customs_residuals/#concept> ;
    skos:closeMatch <http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300011860> ;
    skos:definition "The outer rind of trees. There is an immense variety of barks known in commerce as cinnamon, Peruvian bark, oak bark, quercitron &c. The term *bark* is, however, generally employed to express either Peruvian bark, or oak bark and quercitron, and it is these only we here notice. Peruvian bark (Fr. quinquina; Ger. kronchina; Dutch, china-bast; Span. quina, quenina; Lat. quenina, Cortex Peruvianus). There are several sorts of this bark known in commerce. The first sort is the *pale* bark of the shops. It is the original cinchona of Peru, and is the produce of different varieties of the *Chinchona officinalis* of Linnaeus. [J. R. McCulloch, Dictionary of Commerce and Commercial Navigation (1880), scan p. 195]"@en ;
    skos:editorialNote "Curated fields — source_taxon remove: Generic bark heading; the 1866 bridge treats it as tanning bark."@en ;
